    "abstract": "Random forest regression is a powerful non-parametric method that adapts to local data characteristics through data-driven partitioning, making it effective across diverse application domains. However, the piecewise constant nature of random forest predictions means each partition is predicted independently, ignoring potential smoothness in the underlying function. Particularly in the small data regime, this lack of information sharing across the input space can lead to suboptimal performance.",
    "domain": "Tabular",
    "key_claims": [
      "Introduces probabilistic kernel smoothing as a general approach to equip any learned prediction function with an arbitrary degree of smoothness.",
      "Smoothing procedure captures the variability/uncertainty in tree cut points under resampling of the training inputs.",
      "Investigates out-of-bag performance of random forest ensemble members as effective criterion to select the degree of smoothness.",
      "Smoothed random forest model consistently improves predictive performance across diverse test cases, particularly in data-scarce settings."
    ],
